<Doc
  title="Composition: parts and pipes"
  kicker="Track T — Tools for Composition"
  description="How to build systems out of small replaceable parts, treat flow as a data pipe, and keep side effects at the edges."
  path="/guide/composition"
>
  <p class="lede">
    A Unix tool does one thing and speaks through stdin and stdout. A component here
    does one thing and speaks through a contract. Everything else — how it is
    invoked, where it runs, who owns it — is configuration.
  </p>

  <h2>One part, one job</h2>

  <p>
    The test is not “is it small?” but “can I describe it without <em>and</em>?”.
    A class that validates a transfer <em>and</em> persists it has two reasons to
    change and two failure modes to test. Split it at the <code>and</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Replaceability follows automatically. A part with one job has a narrow interface,
    and a narrow interface has few callers to update.
  </p>

  <h2>The data pipe</h2>

  <p>
    Input arrives through an adapter (HTTP controller, CLI command, queue consumer),
    traverses the core as plain data, and leaves through another adapter. The core
    never knows which adapter called it.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="A use case receives ports, not frameworks"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'PHP', code: phpPipe },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'C#', code: csPipe },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Notice what this class does not import: no HTTP, no ORM, no container. It knows
    two ports named after business concepts, and nothing else. That is the pipe.
  </p>

  <h2>Composition happens at the edge</h2>

  <p>
    Someone has to decide which implementation is real. That decision belongs to a
    composition root — one place, usually the entry point, where infrastructure is
    wired to the core.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Composition root"
    tabs={[{ id: 'php', label: 'PHP', code: phpRoot }]}
  />

  <p>
    In C# that place is <code>Program.cs</code>; in Java, the module or a framework
    configuration class; in C++, <code>main</code>. One file, not scattered through
    the domain.
  </p>

  <Note label="Smell" variant="warn">
    <p>
      If a domain class asks a container for a dependency, composition has leaked
      inward. <code>$container->get(...)</code> inside a use case is a hidden
      parameter and an untestable seam.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <h2>Parts must be replaceable, not just small</h2>

  <p>
    Replaceability requires three things:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>A contract</strong> that describes behaviour in domain terms.</li>
    <li><strong>No leaked types</strong> from the implementation (no <code>QueryBuilder</code> in a signature).</li>
    <li><strong>One implementation per role</strong> in production, and an in-memory one in tests.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The in-memory implementation is the cheapest architecture test you will ever
    write: if the use case cannot run against a fake, it is not composed — it is
    fused to a framework.
  </p>

  <h2>Cycles are the failure signal</h2>

  <p>
    Composition makes dependencies visible. When the graph contains a cycle, two parts
    are actually one, or a responsibility is in the wrong place. Tools that render the
    graph — <code>deptrac</code>, <code>ArchUnit</code>, <code>jdeps</code>,
    <code>include-what-you-use</code> — turn that signal into a build failure.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Composition mechanism</th><th>Dependency graph check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>PHP</td><td>constructor injection, PSR-11 container at the edge</td><td>deptrac</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C#</td><td>constructor injection, <code>IServiceCollection</code></td><td>NetArchTest</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Java</td><td>constructor injection, manual wiring or CDI</td><td>ArchUnit, <code>jdeps</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>C++</td><td>references/pointers in constructors, manual wiring in <code>main</code></td><td>build graph, IWYU</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What composition is not</h2>

  <p>
    It is not “extract an interface for everything”. An interface with one
    implementation and no test fake is ceremony. Composition is justified by a second
    implementation — real or in-memory.
  </p>
</Doc>
